      {
        "id": "S01",
        "title": "Not a perfect storm",
        "headline": "The danger is not only the crises. It is their interactions.",
        "targetSeconds": 60,
        "notes": "Tone: Calm and arresting. Name each crisis plainly, then let the links and the silence do the work.\n\n- Open with familiar crises, not statistics. Name the six words slowly.\n- Let the links draw themselves, one at a time; do not explain every connection.\n- Lawrence et al. reject the \"perfect storm\" metaphor: it implies coincidence, when these crises are causally entangled.\n- Definition (Lawrence et al.): a global polycrisis is the causal entanglement of crises in multiple global systems in ways that significantly degrade humanity's prospects.\n- Hold the silence after \"An entanglement.\" before you move on.\n\nKey facts:\n- Lawrence et al. (2024, p. 1) reject the \"perfect storm\" metaphor because it implies mere coincidence.\n- Their definition (p. 4): interacting crises produce harms different from, and usually greater than, the sum of the harms they would produce separately.",
        "sources": [
          "L",
          "G"
        ],
        "steps": [
