      {
        "id": "S02",
        "title": "From idea to institutions",
        "headline": "An old systems insight, a newer name, and growing institutional use.",
        "targetSeconds": 95,
        "notes": "Tone: Brisk and grounding: this is the credibility scene. Lift a little at the end; the experts agree it is more than a buzzword.\n\n- Separate the thinking from the word: Limits to Growth and systems research are predecessors, not the origin of \"polycrise\".\n- Morin and Kern coined polycrise in Terre-Patrie (1993). Mark Swilling, a South African sociologist, revived it after 2008 for the mutually amplifying effects of climate change, inequality and financial volatility. Jean-Claude Juncker, then European Commission president, used it in 2016 for the EU's entangled crises (stagnation, migration, Brexit).\n- Columbia economic historian Adam Tooze brought it back into the global lexicon (Shutdown, 2021; commentaries, 2022). In parallel, the Cascade Institute (Michael Lawrence, Scott Janzwood and Thomas Homer-Dixon) defined the global polycrisis in 2022 and set out its causal mechanisms in 2024 (Lawrence et al., one of our sources). Davos 2023 made it a buzzword.\n- Earlier polycrises (Lawrence et al.): the 1970s oil shocks and the 2008-09 food, fuel and finance crisis.\n- Institutions at different levels: the EU JRC workshop (maps cascades, identifies interventions); Global Affairs Canada's WPS plan, Crisis Response focus area; the WEF Global Risks Report (risk assessment, not policy); the Cascade Institute at Royal Roads (research).\n- Contested (Delannoy et al., 50 experts): structural roots in capitalism and colonialism; networked shocks from tight coupling; a failure of global governance; a call for conceptual rigour. All four reject \"mere buzzword\". Gambhir et al. add that concentrated power and homogeneity make systems fragile.\n\nKey facts:\n- Delannoy et al. (2026, p. 1, p. 5): four framings among 50 experts; all reject the view that polycrisis is a mere buzzword.\n- Delannoy et al. (2026, p. 2): Swilling (2013) revived the term after the 2008 crisis; Juncker (2016) used it for the EU's concurrent challenges; Columbia University economic historian Adam Tooze reintroduced it to the global lexicon (Tooze 2021, 2022); in parallel the Cascade Institute proposed 'global polycrisis' (Lawrence, Janzwood & Homer-Dixon 2022). Lawrence et al. (2024, p. 2): Tooze and Cascade Institute researchers both used the term in the months after February 2022.\n- Lawrence et al. (2024, p. 5): the 1970s oil shocks and 2008-09 were earlier polycrises.\n- Global Affairs Canada, WPS Implementation Plan 2023-2029, Focus Area 3 (Crisis Response): \"a state of polycrisis\".\n- WEF Global Risks Report 2026 preface: the 2023 edition raised the possibility of a polycrisis (secondhand; direct page fetch blocked).",
        "sources": [
          "D",
          "L",
          "G",
          "R",
          "WEB_EU_JRC",
          "WEB_GAC",
          "WEB_WEF"
        ],
