      {
        "id": "S03",
        "title": "Anatomy of the polycrisis",
        "headline": "Slow stresses, fast triggers, and the ways crises become entangled.",
        "targetSeconds": 120,
        "notes": "Tone: Analytical, with rising tension: more connected, less stable, and less room for error.\n\n- The map: a qualitative synthesis of 41 variables and 132 links, not a simulation. For an audience that knows causal-loop notation, five seconds on the legend is enough.\n- New grammar: shape = role. Squares are slow stresses, circles fast triggers, octagons crises, rings responses (Lawrence et al.; Gambhir et al.).\n- Why now (Lawrence et al. pp. 5-6): hyper-connectivity plus a destabilized Earth system. Say \"current dollars\" and \"projected\".\n- The stability landscape (3a-3c): stresses make the basin shallower, a trigger tips the system out, and the crisis is the system out of equilibrium. Stress × trigger → crisis.\n- Common stresses vs. a common trigger (Lawrence et al. Fig. 6a-b): the invasion of Ukraine hit energy, food, supply chains and politics at once.\n- Then a domino chain and a feedback (lock-in): together, the four make the grammar of crisis interactions.\n\nKey facts:\n- Lawrence et al. (2024, p. 7): a crisis arises when fast triggers combine with slow stresses that have made the basin of stability shallower.\n- Lawrence et al. (2024, p. 5): merchandise trade rose twelve-fold 1980-2022 (current prices), air freight six-fold, internet use from about 0 to 60%; energy imbalance about 1.36 W/m².\n- Lawrence et al. (2024, p. 8, Fig. 6): common stresses, common triggers, interacting stresses, stress-trigger links, crisis impacts on other systems, crisis-crisis interactions.\n- Gambhir et al. (2025, p. 5): the 2022 food-energy crisis as stresses, a common trigger and crises.",
