      {
        "id": "S04",
        "title": "How crises cascade",
        "headline": "A shock in one system can become a crisis somewhere else, if conditions allow.",
        "targetSeconds": 85,
        "notes": "Tone: Concrete and sobering. Slow down on the human cost, then a first note of agency: transmission is not automatic.\n\n- Path A, one keypress per hop: conflict → energy prices → farm inputs → food prices → food insecurity. Not every price rise has only this cause.\n- Historical anchor (Gambhir et al.): in 2022 energy was 40-50% of variable cropping costs in advanced economies, and over 60 million more people faced a food crisis than in 2021.\n- Path B reaches the same households through crops and markets; make no universal climate-to-conflict claim.\n- The ripple: food-price spikes are among the best-documented routes to unrest (Richards et al.: 20 of 41 empirical studies concern conflict); they also feed the cost-of-living crisis (Gambhir et al. Fig. 2) and displacement.\n- Transmission is conditional (Brosig): it depends on functional dependency and fragility. Scheffran: a chain is the product of its links, so one buffered link stops it; tight coupling escalates, and the crisis feeds back.\n- What sets each link: exposure, institutions, income, food reserves, social protection.\n\nKey facts:\n- Gambhir et al. (2025, pp. 3-4): energy 40-50% of variable cropping costs; over 60 million more people in food crisis in 2022 than 2021.\n- Richards et al. (2021, p. 10): of 41 empirical evidence points, 20 concern conflict and 12 emigration.\n- Brosig (2025, pp. 2-5): crises do not spread automatically; conditions and resilience shape transmission.\n- Scheffran (2025, p. 7): a domino chain is interrupted if one sensitivity is zero.",
        "sources": [
          "G",
          "RI",
          "RIS",
          "B",
          "S",
          "L"
        ],
        "steps": [
          {
            "id": "1",
