      {
        "id": "S05",
        "title": "Feedback: the widening lens",
        "headline": "Follow one loop outward and the loops multiply.",
        "targetSeconds": 100,
        "notes": "Tone: Heavier now: the talk's low point. Let the counter climb in silence for a beat; then one honest glimmer, and its erosion.\n\n- R1 first: food insecurity and lost income reinforce each other (Lawrence et al. Fig. 6c).\n- Widen the lens exactly as Lawrence et al. Fig. 9 does, one system per step (9A-9D). The 9C step runs through the climate action gap: their \"weakening of the international climate regime\" (p. 12).\n- The counter counts closed loops in view: structural entanglement in a qualitative sketch, not proof that any loop is active. On the whole map, most loops run through twenty or more links.\n- Lawrence et al. (p. 3): with three or more systems \"the number of combinatorial possibilities explodes\". Richards et al.: 39 variables, 105 links, about 32,000 loops.\n- The twist: until responses entered the map, every loop was reinforcing; Brosig warns that the field over-emphasizes vicious loops.\n- Balancing loops: harm prompts preparedness (B1); shocks prompt diversification (B2). Fiscal and governance strain erode them: Collste et al.'s Crisis Deferral. Inequality blocks cooperation: Deepening Divides.\n\nKey facts:\n- Lawrence et al. (2024, pp. 11-12, Fig. 9): reinforcing loops through nationalist, anti-globalization ideology; \"not inevitable\".\n- Richards et al. (2021, p. 13): 39 variables, 105 links and 32,000 feedback loops (counted in Vensim).\n- Brosig (2025, p. 5): polycrisis research tends to emphasize, maybe over-emphasize, aggravating feedback loops.\n- Collste et al. (2025, pp. 4, 8): Deepening Divides; Crisis Deferral.",
        "sources": [
          "L",
          "RI",
          "B",
          "C"
        ],
        "steps": [
          {
            "id": "1",
