        "title": "Beneath the loops: Anthropocene traps",
        "headline": "Under the loops sit slower traps: yesterday's solutions that now lock us in.",
        "targetSeconds": 60,
        "notes": "Tone: The deepest layer: quiet and unhurried. Then turn the corner; a trap you can name is a trap you can begin to escape.\n\n- Traps (Søgaard Jørgensen et al.): once-adaptive practices (growth, connectivity, technology) that became maladaptive and hard to escape.\n- Of 182 possible pairings, 87 were judged non-neutral: 81 amplifying, 6 dampening. Expert judgements, not measurements; do not quote the main text's 83/87.\n- Influence vs. exposure is Lawrence et al.'s out-degree / in-degree heuristic; degree is not proof of leverage.\n- Phases (Table 1): 12 of 14 possibly in an advanced trapping phase (8 trapping, 4 masking-to-trapping), 2 scaling-to-masking; 10 of 14 growing. The paper's own phase example is antibiotic resistance (Fig. 1b); fossil-fuel infrastructure is the indicator for infrastructure lock-in (onset of trapping, p. 9).\n- Trap-to-map correspondences are our own reading: most traps have a counterpart on our map; existential technology (nuclear weapons) and biosphere disconnect do not.\n- Root traps (p. 8): division, short-termism, overconsumption, technological autonomy. Land the turn: recognizing traps is the first capacity for escaping them (pp. 11-12).\n\nKey facts:\n- Søgaard Jørgensen et al. (2024, p. 1, p. 4): 14 traps in three groups; 12 possibly in an advanced phase; 10 growing.\n- Supplement Table S4 (pp. 23-29): 87 non-neutral directed interactions, 81 amplifying or likely amplifying, 6 dampening or likely dampening.\n- From Table S4: short-termism amplifies 12 traps; overshoot is amplified by 11. (The main text, p. 8, says the root traps each cause eight; quote the table.)",
        "sources": [
          "J",
          "JS",
          "L"
        ],
        "steps": [
          {
