      {
        "id": "S07",
        "title": "What this means for environmental professionals",
        "headline": "A polycrisis lens changes the questions we ask at work.",
        "targetSeconds": 70,
        "notes": "Tone: The turn toward agency: warmer and more direct. Speak to the audience as fellow practitioners.\n\n- The turn: from diagnosis to what we do at work.\n- Conservation: restoration touches land, water, livelihoods, rights and climate feedbacks.\n- Adaptation and disaster risk: reduce exposure and vulnerability, not just the hazard; watch health systems and displacement.\n- Policy, governance and community: who can act, which incentives dominate, whose costs are exported; inequality and polarization shape cooperation.\n- Regenerative business: supply concentration, fossil lock-in, cost-of-living exposure, the energy appetite of new technology.\n- The lens complements domain expertise; it does not replace it.\n\nKey facts:\n- Gambhir et al. (2025): system architecture, political economy, non-human life, diverse evidence and uncertainty.\n- Liu and Renn (2025): cooperative, integrative and inclusive risk governance.",
        "sources": [
          "G",
          "R",
          "B",
          "D"
        ],
