      {
        "id": "S08",
        "title": "Finding leverage",
        "headline": "Look for places where a feasible change interrupts several harmful pathways.",
        "targetSeconds": 85,
        "notes": "Tone: Constructive and precise: from diagnosis to choices. Being honest about fixes that fail earns the hope that follows.\n\n- Leverage is a working hypothesis, not something this network can calculate; high degree is not proof.\n- Four tests, one keypress each: interrupt transmission? a shared vulnerability? backfire or a shifted burden? capacity for deeper change? Each map example is a sketch, not a finding.\n- Fixes that fail: replacing Russian gas with new LNG infrastructure can prolong fossil dependence (Gambhir et al. Table 3); efficiency alone can rebound into more demand (Collste et al., Jevons).\n- Break a link (Scheffran: a chain is the product of its links); strengthen negative feedbacks, add buffers and firebreaks, and weigh resilience alongside efficiency (Lawrence et al. p. 13).\n- Work at the root: short-termism amplifies 12 traps (Table S4). Collste et al.: the deepest leverage is in goals and paradigms, from efficiency toward robustness.\n\nKey facts:\n- Lawrence et al. (2024, p. 13): address system architecture, not just events.\n- Gambhir et al. (2025, p. 11, Table 3): alternative gas supplies can lock in new LNG infrastructure.\n- Collste et al. (2025, p. 8): rebound effects; efficiency versus robustness.\n- Scheffran (2025, p. 7): a domino chain is interrupted if one sensitivity is zero.",
        "sources": [
          "L",
          "S",
          "G",
          "C",
          "J",
          "B"
        ],
        "steps": [
          {
