      {
        "id": "S09",
        "title": "From intervention to systemic effects",
        "headline": "The connections that spread harm can carry relief, with trade-offs.",
        "targetSeconds": 95,
        "notes": "Tone: Hope with honesty. Let the teal relief travel before you speak; name the trade-offs plainly, without deflating the audience.\n\n- A: diversify supply and response options; this may reduce some routes for cascading price shocks.\n- B2 closes: energy shocks prompt diversification, which damps the next shock (Gambhir et al. p. 6). R13 opens if diversification means new fossil supply (Table 3).\n- B: restoration designed with local ecological knowledge and community needs may relieve water and food pressures; the link to tipping feedbacks is a synthesis, not a finding.\n- Pulses are hypotheses, not quantities: say \"may reduce\" and \"can support\", never \"will solve\".\n- Trade-offs to name: cost, land use, rights, displacement, scale mismatch, uneven benefits.\n\nKey facts:\n- Both interventions are conceptual candidates synthesized from the supplied papers; their paths are not quantitatively validated.\n- Scheffran (2025): interventions can shift vicious circles toward virtuous ones, conditionally.\n- Brosig (2025): resilience shapes transmission.",
        "sources": [
          "G",
          "S",
          "B",
          "R",
          "J",
          "C"
        ],
        "steps": [
          {
