      {
        "id": "S10",
        "title": "Interconnectedness is also our opportunity",
        "headline": "The same connections that spread harm can help spread resilience.",
        "targetSeconds": 70,
        "notes": "Tone: Uplift and resolve. Slow down for the three closing lines, then stop, and let the relief keep moving behind you.\n\n- Urgency without fatalism; big transformations remain necessary.\n- Reinforcing loops are neutral amplifiers: push one the other way and it amplifies recovery (Scheffran: vicious to virtuous circles; Brosig: tipping can work for positive change).\n- Morin, via Delannoy et al.: a crisis releases forces of breakdown and of regeneration at once: a hinge.\n- Five capacities (Søgaard Jørgensen et al.): recognize traps, measure and foresee, reorganize and innovate, prepare for surprise, navigate conflict.\n- Finish with the three lines and a thank-you, then stop. The relief keeps travelling behind you through questions.\n\nKey facts:\n- This close is a synthesized argument, not a prediction of particular results.\n- Søgaard Jørgensen et al. (2024, pp. 11-12): five capacities for navigating traps.\n- Delannoy et al. (2026, pp. 10-11): Morin's view of crisis as both danger and doorway to renewal.",
        "sources": [
          "S",
          "D",
          "J",
          "B",
          "L"
        ],
        "steps": [
          {
            "id": "1",
            "label": "A virtuous circle",
            "at": 0,
            "cue": "Virtuous circle: R1 in reverse, relief circulating.",
            "map": {
              "camera": {
                "fit": [
